
Put a $5 chip on every black square. Then I put a $5 chip on every split
that is beside the black square and provides a red number.

/* The layout generally looks like this : 

    -------------------------------------------------------------+
   / |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
  /  | 3r | 6b | 9r | 12r| 15b| 18r| 21r| 24b| 27r| 30r| 33b| 36r|
 /   |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+ 00 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+
|    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+----+ 2b | 5r | 8b | 11b| 14r| 17b| 20b| 23r| 26b| 29b| 32r| 35b|
|    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+  0 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+
 \   |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
  \  | 1r | 4b | 7r | 10b| 13b| 16r| 19r| 22b| 25r| 28b| 31b| 34r|
   \ |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
    -------------------------------------------------------------+

     */



So if we place an X on every black square then we see this : 


    -------------------------------------------------------------+
   / |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
  /  | 3r | 6b | 9r | 12r| 15b| 18r| 21r| 24b| 27r| 30r| 33b| 36r|
 /   |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |
+ 00 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+
|    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |
+----+ 2b | 5r | 8b | 11b| 14r| 17b| 20b| 23r| 26b| 29b| 32r| 35b|
|    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+  0 +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+
 \   |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |
  \  | 1r | 4b | 7r | 10b| 13b| 16r| 19r| 22b| 25r| 28b| 31b| 34r|
   \ |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
    -------------------------------------------------------------+


Now place a bet on every split that adds in a red number : 


    -------------------------------------------------------------+
   / |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
  /  | 3r X 6b | 9r | 12rX 15b| 18r| 21rX 24b| 27r| 30rX 33b| 36r|
 /   |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |
+ 00 +----+----+--X-+----+----+--X-+----+----+----+----+----+--X-+
|    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |
+----+ 2b X 5r | 8b | 11bX 14r| 17b| 20b| 23rX 26b| 29bX 32r| 35b|
|    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+  0 +----+----+----+----+----+----+--X-+----+----+----+----+----+
 \   |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |
  \  | 1r X 4b | 7r X 10b| 13bX 16r| 19rX 22b| 25rX 28b| 31bX 34r|
   \ |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
    -------------------------------------------------------------+

In this way we have every black number covered as well as a good portion
of the red numbers.  In fact all of them except for 19 red.  Does this
matter in any way? Nope.

Lets look at the probability of a "win" here.  We have 38 numbers on the
wheel when we include the 36 regular numbers and the 0 and double 00. I
can tell you that the little bouncing ball is blind and does not see if
there is a red landing spot or a green or black. That silly thing has no
real clue at all. So firstly lets consider the classic red black issue
where some dumb mook only ever bets on red and black and also the idiot
plays with a Martingale betting strategy. That always wins right? If you
have enough of a bankroll and the table has no limits then sure. Good
luck with that.

There are 18 red numbers and 18 black numbers with a wheel of 38 total
possible results and thus : 

             ρ(Rw) = 18 / 38 = 0.47368421

Where this means probability of a "win" for red numbers. So here we see
the result is a bit better than 47% and surely anyone can see the same
result for black.  In general the house always wins with a slight edge
that gives 52.6% to the casino. However wait. What if we cover all of
the black numbers as above and toss in the splits that provide a red
number?


    -------------------------------------------------------------+
   / |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
  /  | 3r X 6b | 9r | 12rX 15b| 18r| 21rX 24b| 27r| 30rX 33b| 36r|
 /   |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |    |  X |    |
+ 00 +----+----+--X-+----+----+--X-+----+----+----+----+----+--X-+
|    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |  X |    |  X |
+----+ 2b X 5r | 8b | 11bX 14r| 17b| 20b| 23rX 26b| 29bX 32r| 35b|
|    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
+  0 +----+----+----+----+----+----+--X-+----+----+----+----+----+
 \   |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |    |  X |    |  X |  X |    |
  \  | 1r X 4b | 7r X 10b| 13bX 16r| 19rX 22b| 25rX 28b| 31bX 34r|
   \ |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |
    -------------------------------------------------------------+

The bets on the table are ( going by row order for black ) with the red
neighbour splits : 

          black       red
            6          3
           15         12
           24         21
           33         30
            2          5
            8          9  Note the red neighbour is on the first row
           11         14
           17         18  Also an upwards split towards the first row
           20         19  Downwards split to third row
           26         23
           29         32
           35         36  Again an upwards split
            4          1
           10          7
           13         16
           22         19
           28         25
           31         34

That is the full list of all black numbers with the red split neighbours
with no consideration to the results from a roulette wheel.  We have two
situations or questions : what is the probability of a "win" and to what
degree is the "win" actually worth winning?  Is it possible, in fact is
it probable that a "win" is a financial loss?  Yes. The numbers are very
clear here and easy to simulate with a bit of software.  However firstly
we need the basic win/loss probabilities.  The most easy thing on the 
table ( no pun intended ) is where are the losers?  Clearly the zero
squares are both losers and that means 0 and 00 green numbers are going
to wipe out the whole table.  I have seen that many times.  Also 27 red
will wipe out the table. That means there are 38 numbers on the table
and we cover 35 of them which is a hell of a number. The probability of
a "win" is 0.92105263. Ponder that. A 92% chance of a win. However is
that really a win?  Sort of depends on what we landed on. We know that
every single bet we made on a black square will result in a direct 35:1
type pay off as well as an additional 17:1 for a split. Are there any
numbers that are different? Of course. All of the red numbers are less
of a payoff by a large margin.

But wait.

That 35 factor pay off with the added 17 is only for a black number. We
can says that black numbers matter here. Almost every red number is very
similar to a Black Jack surrender in that you get the 17:1 pay off and
lose everything else.  For the sake of being detailed lets look at the
actual loss and win numbers for this crazy sort of roulette bet :


       number          pay out factor             With $5 chips
    ------------------------------------------------------------
            0          total loss                             0
           00          total loss                             0
       red  1          17                                    85
            2          35 + 17                              260
       r    3          17                                    85
            4          35 + 17                              260
       r    5          17                                    85
            6          35 + 17                              260
       r    7          17                                    85
            8          35 + 17                              260
       r    9          17                                    85
           10          35 + 17                              260
           11          35 + 17                              260
       r   12          17                                    85
           13          35 + 17                              260
       r   14          17                                    85
           15          35 + 17                              260
       r   16          17                                    85
           17          35 + 17                              260
       r   18          17                                    85
       r   19          17 + 17 double split red             170
           20          35 + 17                              260
       r   21          17                                    85
           22          35 + 17                              260
       r   23          17                                    85
           24          35 + 17                              260
       r   25          17                                    85
           26          35 + 17                              260
       r   27          total loss
           28          35 + 17                              260
           29          35 + 17                              260
       r   30          17                                    85
           31          35 + 17                              260
       r   32          17                                    85
           33          35 + 17                              260
       r   34          17                                    85
           35          35 + 17                              260
       r   36          17                                    85

How many of the above are maximum value 260? Well those are the black
numbers. So we already know the percentage there is 47.368 or at best
a little worse than fifty percent. Everything else is very likely a
loss.  There are a total of 35 bets on the table and the very best
one may hope for is a 47% win. Every other possibility is a loss and we
see the 0 and double 00 as a disaster. The same is true for the red 27.
However lets look at the probabilities : 

          disaster =  3/38 = 0.07894736 =  7.89%
            red 19 =  1/38 = 0.02631578 =  2.63%
             split = 16/38 = 0.42105263 = 42.11%
           perfect = 18/38 = 0.47368421 = 47.37%

The sum of those probabilities has to be 1 exactly unless we consider
the bizarre situation where the roulette balls jumps out of the track
and onto the floor somewhere. Yes I have seen that also. However that
results in nothing happening except for a scramble around to find the
little white ball and then toss it in the trash. Sort of like dice at
a craps table one should, in theory, throw away a little red die that
made a leap for the floor.  That never happens.  Regardless the total
bet on the table has to be $170 in order to cover all those numbers
and the splits.  There is a 42.11% chance that you lose a large piece
of that with only $85 payout. A very very thin 2.63% that you hit the
red 19 which is a double split 34:1 for precisely $170 payout which
means nothing happens for you. So a dumb question that is perfectly 
unreasonable is what happens if a schmuck walks into a casino with a
bankroll of $2000 and then plays this situation? The math will always
tell us that the house wins and over any period of time you go broke.
However, how often does that happen?  Over what period of time? When
can we expect the schmuck to walk away to the bar and just drink on
the remains of a credit card?  Well a good criteria here would be to
say they the walk away happens after $1000 is won or lost. Even more
likely is that the $1000 win amount is the walk away criteria and no
other option than to go broke.  Should be easy to simulate with some
decent RNG source.

For the sake of another discussion we will use the Mersenne Twister
as the PRNG here.  Mostly because it is entirely repeatable. As you
may know we can also use drand48() with a set seed value and get a
very repeatable sequence of random numbers also.  However unless we
use a radiation decay source then a pseudo random number sequence
is all we can expect.




